/* Original firmware-development learning path. Stable IDs preserve existing bookmarks. */
function topic(title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,explanation,worked,f401,h7,troubleshoot) {
 lesson(0,title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,h7.join(' '));
 const l=lessons[lessons.length-1];
 l.detail={id:l.id,explanation,worked,boards:{f401,h7},expected,troubleshoot,questions:[]};
}
topic('From product requirements to a firmware system',
 'Firmware translates an external requirement into bounded interactions between a processor, memory, peripherals and physical devices. Begin with inputs, outputs, failure behavior and timing before choosing functions or tasks. A system can be logically correct yet unsafe or unusable if it responds too late or reports stale data as current.',
 'A temperature monitor samples once per second, displays a fresh value within 100 ms of completion and signals a sensor failure after three missed samples. These are separate contracts that can be measured independently.',
 '/* A contract represented in data, independent of a specific MCU. */\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\ntypedef struct {\n    int32_t value_milli_c;\n    uint32_t acquired_ms;\n    bool valid;\n} sample_t;\n/* Define who writes, who reads and how ownership changes. */',
 ['Write inputs, outputs, update rates and invalid-data behavior for a monitor.','Draw processor, sensor, memory, display and power/reset boundaries.','Choose one measurable acceptance test for each requirement.','Separate hardware-independent policy from peripheral access.'],
 'A requirements table specifies timing, units, freshness and failure response; every output has a defined owner.',
 'Design a water-level alarm: include sensor disconnection, startup behavior and an alarm-clear rule.',
 'How do you choose between polling, interrupts and DMA?',
 'Start with rate, latency, CPU budget and ownership complexity. Polling is simple for bounded low-rate checks, interrupts suit asynchronous events, and DMA can reduce per-byte CPU work but needs explicit completion and buffer ownership.',
 ['Name the data contract at each boundary. Millivolts and raw ADC counts are different units; a boolean success code does not communicate acquisition time or freshness.','Keep product policy testable without registers. A hardware adapter supplies samples and errors, while application logic decides when a sample is too old and how an alarm behaves.'],
 'For a one-second sample period and a three-miss rule, specify whether the alarm begins three seconds after the last success or after the third scheduled failed attempt. Tests need one interpretation, not an ambiguous phrase.',
 ['Create a F401 scratch project and represent a synthetic sample with explicit units and timestamp.','Update it once per second and inspect valid/fresh status without a physical sensor.','Document which module owns writes and how another module reads a consistent snapshot.'],
 ['Repeat the same policy on H743 without changing units or freshness thresholds.','Keep clock-dependent timestamp generation in the hardware adapter.','Explain which requirements remain unchanged despite a faster core.'],
 'If behavior changes between boards, check timebase units and adapter contracts before changing product thresholds.');
topic('C objects, pointers and lifetime on a microcontroller',
 'An object has storage, type, size and lifetime. A pointer value is useful only while it refers to a live object that supports the intended access. Stack, static and allocated storage have different ownership rules. MCU memory maps add access restrictions, but ordinary C lifetime rules still apply.',
 'A UART driver storing a caller pointer must know whether the data remains valid until asynchronous completion. A local array that returns before DMA finishes cannot be treated like a permanently owned transmit buffer.',
 '#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\nbool read_u16_le(const uint8_t *p, size_t n, uint16_t *out) {\n    if (!p || !out || n < 2U) return false;\n    *out = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)p[0] | ((uint16_t)p[1] << 8));\n    return true;\n}',
 ['Identify size and lifetime of a local array, a static array and a pointer.','Decode a two-byte value without casting an unaligned byte pointer to a wider type.','Test null pointers, one-byte input and two-byte input.','Document who owns asynchronous buffers until completion.'],
 'Bytes 0x34,0x12 decode to 0x1234; short input rejects without touching the output.',
 'Design a transmit API that either copies the input or explicitly borrows it until completion.',
 'Why avoid casting a packet byte array to a C struct?',
 'Alignment, padding, endianness and representation can differ. Explicit field decoding makes the wire format independent of the compiler layout and permits bounds checks before each access.',
 ['sizeof an array in its declaring scope measures its full storage; a function parameter declared as an array is adjusted to a pointer and does not retain that capacity. Pass lengths explicitly.','A wider integer load from arbitrary bytes can also violate alignment or aliasing rules. Byte-wise decoding makes the intended representation clear and works across targets.'],
 'With input {0x34,0x12} and n=1 the function returns false and preserves *out. With n=2 it returns true and sets 4660. Use a sentinel output to check the rejection contract.',
 ['Run the byte-decoder cases in a F401 project with a sentinel output.','Inspect sizeof for an actual array and for a pointer using the compiler/debugger.','Review one function for pointers that escape their owning scope.'],
 ['Run the identical byte tests on H743 and keep the same wire format.','Inspect a buffer used by asynchronous UART/DMA and write down its completion-based lifetime.','Avoid relying on an M7 unaligned-access behavior as permission for an invalid C cast.'],
 'If output changes on a rejected case, inspect partial writes and test initialization. If a pointer looks valid but data changes later, investigate lifetime and buffer reuse.');
topic('Bits, registers and safe hardware access',
 'Peripheral registers encode fields and side effects, not ordinary variables. Use unsigned masks and device headers, distinguish read/write configuration from write-one-to-clear status, and follow documented sequencing. volatile is appropriate for memory-mapped access but cannot make an unsafe read-modify-write sequence correct.',
 'Two writers updating different bits of one output register can still lose changes if each reads an old value and writes a modified copy. A hardware atomic set/reset register can avoid that specific register race when the device provides it.',
 '#include <stdint.h>\n/* Pure field manipulation example; not a universal register API. */\nuint32_t replace_field(uint32_t old) {\n    const uint32_t mask = 3U << 4;\n    return (old & ~mask) | (2U << 4);\n}\n/* For actual registers, consult access semantics before RMW. */',
 ['Decode a field mask and calculate which bits change.','Find GPIO mode, output and set/reset register semantics in the exact reference manual.','Use HAL or documented atomic GPIO writes for the first experiment.','Identify one status register where ordinary RMW is inappropriate.'],
 'Replacing bits 5:4 with binary 10 changes no unrelated bits; the hardware procedure uses the correct access semantics.',
 'Explain how an interrupt can interfere with a main-loop read-modify-write on a shared register.',
 'Why are bitfields risky for portable MMIO code?',
 'Layout, access width and generated read-modify-write operations depend on implementation. Use vendor definitions and explicitly documented accesses, inspecting generated instructions when necessary.',
 ['Unsigned constants make shift intent explicit. Verify shift counts are within the width and that the value fits its field. A mask that selects the wrong register field can be perfectly valid C and still configure invalid hardware.','A read of a status register may clear events or consume data. Register inspection is an operation with semantics; choose software snapshots where repeated debugger reads would disturb the peripheral.'],
 'For old=0xA5, clearing mask 0x30 gives 0x85 and setting 0x20 gives 0xA5. For old=0xFF, the result is 0xEF. Test multiple old values so an unchanged first example does not hide a broken implementation.',
 ['Compute field results in F401 RAM variables first.','Use a spare output selected from the schematic and compare atomic GPIO set/reset with an owned output state.','Do not write arbitrary status bits as an experiment.'],
 ['Repeat the pure field calculation on H743.','Find its documented GPIO set/reset semantics rather than copying raw F401 addresses.','List which register accesses in your driver have side effects and which can safely be sampled.'],
 'If adjacent behavior changes, verify mask width, field position and shared writers. A successful compiler build does not validate a register operation.');
topic('Electrical interfaces and a safe board workbench',
 'Digital signals need compatible voltage levels, a reference ground and defined drive behavior. Open-drain outputs need pull-ups; push-pull outputs must not fight another driver. A USB connector, UART header and SWD interface can carry different signals even when physically similar.',
 'A 3.3 V UART adapter can communicate with a compatible MCU UART when TX crosses to RX and grounds are connected. RS-232 signaling is a different electrical interface and must not connect directly to an MCU pin.',
 'Workbench record\nBoard and revision:\nPower connector / supply limit:\nSignal voltage and reference ground:\nPin name / connector / alternate function:\nProbe ground and measurement point:\nExpected idle level and waveform:',
 ['Read the board schematic and identify power, ground, reset and debug connectors.','Verify signal voltage compatibility from the exact pin/device documentation.','Choose an unused GPIO and observe it with an appropriate probe.','Record idle and active levels before connecting an external module.'],
 'A wiring table matches schematic signal names to physical connectors and documents compatible voltage levels.',
 'Explain what changes when connecting an open-drain I2C signal instead of push-pull UART TX.',
 'Why can a logic analyzer show no valid data even if firmware is running?',
 'Possible causes include missing ground reference, incorrect pin, threshold, baud/framing or actual absent transitions. Correlate register setup with a measured signal at the MCU pin and connector.',
 ['Do not infer tolerance from a board name. Pin voltage limits, injection current restrictions and mode-dependent behavior belong to the exact datasheet. A five-volt-powered module may still drive a five-volt output.','Probe the smallest useful boundary first: board supply, reset, MCU pin and then external connector. An intermittent wire or routing jumper can mimic a driver defect.'],
 'For a signal that idles high but never shows a start bit, verify that the observed node is TX rather than RX and that the application reached its transmit call. If the MCU pin toggles but the module input does not, investigate the electrical path.',
 ['Use F401RE schematic to map one GPIO and the documented debug USB route.','Measure the output high/low levels with suitable equipment.','Record whether an external UART adapter supplies power or only signal/reference connections.'],
 ['Map H743ZI2 connectors and select a free pin from its own schematic.','Check board routing and alternate-function availability for that exact pin.','Repeat measurements without assuming the same connector position carries the F401 signal.'],
 'If a module heats, supply collapses or two outputs contend, disconnect the interface and correct the wiring/power contract before further firmware tests.');
topic('State machines instead of blocking application loops',
 'A state machine separates what the application is doing from when an event occurs. Each step performs bounded work, records state and returns. This allows timeouts, user input and background communication to progress without long delay calls. Define transitions and invalid events explicitly.',
 'A button-controlled sampler can move through IDLE, WAITING and READY. The application starts a sensor request, returns to other work, then handles completion or timeout on a later step.',
 '#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\ntypedef enum { IDLE, WAITING, READY, FAILED } state_t;\nstatic state_t state;\nstatic uint32_t started;\nvoid step(uint32_t now, bool start, bool done) {\n    switch (state) {\n    case IDLE: if (start) { started=now; state=WAITING; } break;\n    case WAITING:\n        if (done) state=READY;\n        else if ((uint32_t)(now-started) >= 100U) state=FAILED;\n        break;\n    default: break; /* Define explicit reset/consume transitions in your app. */\n    }\n}',
 ['Draw the transition table and assign one owner of state.','Drive the example with synthetic millisecond timestamps and events.','Test completion before, at and after the timeout; specify precedence when both occur.','Add consume/reset transitions without doing blocking work in step.'],
 'The example gives completion precedence when done and timeout are observed together; no-event calls return promptly.',
 'Extend FAILED with a bounded retry count and prove a stuck sensor cannot stop the heartbeat.',
 'When would a superloop be enough instead of an RTOS?',
 'When bounded cooperative work, event handling and deadlines fit a comprehensible single-loop schedule. Measure latency and complexity; an RTOS helps with concurrency structure but does not eliminate ownership or timing analysis.',
 ['Unsigned elapsed subtraction can handle timer wrap when the real interval is within the counter range. Define the time unit and the maximum service gap. A state-machine timeout cannot be timely if the state machine itself is not serviced.','Decide event semantics: a level can be read repeatedly, whereas a queued event should be consumed once. Debounce physical buttons before using edges as application events.'],
 'Start at timestamp 0xFFFFFFF0 and inspect at 0x00000054. The unsigned difference is 100, so the timeout condition is true even across wrap. Test this arithmetically before waiting for a hardware tick to wrap.',
 ['Drive the F401 state machine from HAL_GetTick in the main loop while a separate heartbeat continues.','Inject a synthetic done flag and compare success with a deliberately absent completion.','Measure the longest main-loop service interval under communication load.'],
 ['Use the same state machine on H743 with the same millisecond contract.','Keep ISR callbacks as event producers; make the main loop the sole transition owner.','If porting to tasks later, retain explicit event ownership rather than allowing several tasks to mutate state.'],
 'If timeout is late, inspect loop service latency. If transitions repeat unexpectedly, define when start/done are consumed and whether they are pulses or persistent levels.');
topic('Startup code, the vector table and the linker contract',
 'Before main, startup establishes the runtime environment: initial stack selection, reset entry, initialized-data copying, zero initialization and system/library setup as required by the toolchain. The vector table connects reset and exceptions to code. The linker script defines where code and storage actually live.',
 'A global initialized to 7 must have its RAM runtime value established before main. If a custom boot path jumps into application code without the expected startup sequence, C assumptions can fail even though flash programming succeeded.',
 '/* Inspect your generated startup file and linker script. */\n/* Conceptual sequence, not a replacement startup implementation: */\n/* initial MSP from vector[0] -> Reset_Handler\n   -> copy .data -> clear .bss -> runtime/system setup -> main */\nvolatile unsigned initialized_value = 7U;\nvolatile unsigned zero_value;',
 ['Locate Reset_Handler, vector table, .data and .bss in the generated project.','Break at reset entry and then at main; inspect globals at each stage.','Identify flash load and RAM execution addresses in the linker script/map.','Document the actual startup order used by your generated toolchain.'],
 'At main, initialized_value is 7 and zero_value is 0; the startup code explains how those values arrived.',
 'Explain what must be different for an application linked behind a bootloader.',
 'Why is jumping directly to main generally not a valid bootloader handoff?',
 'The application expects its startup/runtime environment, stack, vectors and interrupt/peripheral state to satisfy a defined contract. Jumping to main skips that setup. Design and validate a device-specific handoff or reset-based boot flow.',
 ['The exact startup order can vary by toolchain and generated system code. Read the actual assembly/C implementation instead of assuming a generic sequence is copied verbatim.','A no-init or retained region must be deliberately excluded from ordinary initialization and validated before use. Uninitialized retained bytes are not automatically a valid crash record.'],
 'A four-byte .data object normally needs a flash initialization value and four bytes of RAM, while a four-byte .bss object needs RAM but no four-byte nonzero load payload. Startup and linker symbols connect these two views.',
 ['Use F401 generated startup assembly and inspect the initial stack pointer and Reset_Handler vector.','Single-step the relevant initialization path where practical and inspect globals at main.','Compare the vector location with SCB VTOR.'],
 ['Use the H743 generated startup and linker file, recording which RAM region holds .data/.bss and the stack.','Inspect initialization for any extra RAM sections introduced by your project.','For dual-core parts, keep each core image/startup contract explicit.'],
 'If globals are wrong at main, inspect startup execution, section copy/clear bounds and linker symbols before blaming a random application write.');
topic('Clock, reset and power-state bring-up',
 'Clock configuration couples oscillator sources, PLLs, bus dividers, peripheral clocks, flash latency and power/voltage constraints. Reset can affect different domains differently. Establish a simple valid clock baseline before raising performance or enabling low-power modes.',
 'A UART baud error may originate in an incorrect peripheral clock assumption. A slow SysTick can make every application timeout wrong even when a terminal still displays occasional messages.',
 'Bring-up measurements\nActual oscillator / source:\nCore and bus clocks:\nPeripheral kernel clock:\nFlash and power configuration:\nMeasured timer period:\nReset reason captured before clear:',
 ['Generate a conservative device-valid clock configuration using board-selected Cube settings.','Verify one timer output or GPIO period against an independent measurement.','Check UART bit timing separately from the core clock.','Capture reset reason early and document which code clears it.'],
 'Measured timing agrees with the declared clock tree; timeout units and reset identity are reproducible.',
 'Create a failure tree for “boots with debugger, fails after power cycle”.',
 'Why can a peripheral clock differ from SystemCoreClock?',
 'Bus dividers and independent peripheral/kernel clock muxes can select different sources. SystemCoreClock describes the core clock, not every peripheral timing input; derive timing from the actual selected peripheral clock.',
 ['Validate configuration against the exact device operating limits and generated initialization sequence. A frequency that works briefly does not establish voltage, flash or thermal correctness.','Separate clock availability from frequency: waiting forever for an absent external oscillator is a different failure from selecting the wrong divider. Use a defined startup failure policy.'],
 'A one-millisecond software tick observed every two real milliseconds doubles real timeout duration. Verify the independent timing reference before assuming the timeout constant or parser is wrong.',
 ['Record F401RE board oscillator routing and generated clock choices.','Measure a known timer period and UART pattern after a cold boot.','Compare reset flags for normal reset and a deliberate software reset in an isolated test.'],
 ['Record H743 power/clock setup from generated initialization; do not transplant F401 PLL/divider values.','Verify peripheral kernel muxes for the UART/timer used by your labs.','Repeat independent measurements with the debugger disconnected after programming.'],
 'If startup stalls in oscillator readiness, check board oscillator population/routing. If timing is scaled uniformly, verify actual source and divider assumptions before changing delays.');
topic('GPIO inputs, debouncing and event boundaries',
 'A GPIO input is an electrical level, not a clean user event. Pull configuration prevents floating inputs, debouncing separates mechanical chatter from intended actions, and edge detection converts a stable level change into a single event. Keep electrical polarity and application meaning separate.',
 'A pushbutton can generate several transitions within milliseconds. Counting every EXTI edge as a separate press can turn one physical action into multiple menu selections.',
 '#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\nstatic bool candidate, stable;\nstatic uint32_t changed;\nbool debounce(bool raw, uint32_t now) {\n    if (raw != candidate) { candidate=raw; changed=now; }\n    if (candidate != stable && (uint32_t)(now-changed) >= 20U) {\n        stable=candidate;\n        return stable; /* rising stable edge only */\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n/* Initialize candidate/stable from the initial sampled level. */',
 ['Determine input polarity and pull configuration from the board schematic.','Feed the debounce function a synthetic chatter sequence before connecting the physical button.','Poll with a bounded service interval and count stable press events.','Test held input, release and fast repeated presses against the specified debounce contract.'],
 'A held button produces one rising stable event, not one event per loop iteration. Transitions shorter than the stability interval are filtered.',
 'Add a long-press event without blocking the main loop or producing repeated press events.',
 'Should every external input use the same debounce delay?',
 'No. Mechanical characteristics, noise, service interval and response requirements differ. Measure the input and specify a stability rule; a 20 ms example is a teaching choice, not a universal requirement.',
 ['A pull-up does not imply that logical one means pressed; a button wired to ground is commonly active-low. Convert electrical level into a semantic pressed value in the adapter.','A polling debounce algorithm assumes sufficiently frequent sampling. If the loop is blocked longer than intended, brief presses may be missed and recognition latency increases.'],
 'For raw transitions at 0,3,7 ms followed by a stable pressed level from 7 ms, the first recognition is at or after 27 ms with this 20 ms rule, depending on poll timing. Record service interval as part of the expected result.',
 ['Read F401RE user-button wiring and convert its actual active level to pressed.','Count accepted events in RAM while pressing, holding and releasing.','Use a synthetic timestamp sequence to verify wrap and chatter tests.'],
 ['Read H743ZI2 user-button wiring instead of assuming F401 polarity or pin.','Run the same pure debounce tests and measure loop service latency.','If using EXTI, keep its handler short and apply event/debounce policy in the owning application context.'],
 'If a held input repeats, inspect edge consumption and initialization. If it oscillates untouched, check pull configuration, wiring and signal noise before lengthening software delays.');
topic('Serial protocols: framing, parsing and backpressure',
 'UART transports bytes; your protocol defines messages. Specify framing, byte order, maximum length, error checks and recovery after malformed input. Backpressure or an overload policy is required when producers can outrun consumers. A parser should consume partial input incrementally and avoid trusting a received length before validation.',
 'A command channel can use newline-delimited ASCII for readability, with a fixed maximum line length. An overlong line must be discarded through the next delimiter so its remaining tail is not accidentally interpreted as a new valid command.',
 '#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\nstatic char line[32];\nstatic size_t used;\nstatic bool dropping;\nbool feed_char(char ch) {\n    if (ch == \'\\n\') {\n        bool ready = !dropping && used > 0;\n        if (ready) line[used] = \'\\0\';\n        used=0; dropping=false;\n        return ready; /* consume line before calling again */\n    }\n    if (dropping) return false;\n    if (used >= sizeof line - 1U) { dropping=true; used=0; return false; }\n    line[used++]=ch; return false;\n}\n/* Contract here treats CR as ordinary data; define CRLF policy explicitly. */',
 ['Specify encoding, delimiter, maximum payload and CRLF behavior.','Feed one byte at a time and test fragmented/concatenated commands.','Test maximum length, overlength discard and valid recovery after newline.','Define who owns the completed line until consumption.'],
 'Up to 31 payload bytes fit with a terminator; overlength input is discarded to newline and the following valid command can be accepted.',
 'Extend the parser with an explicit result enum for incomplete, complete and rejected, plus an overload counter.',
 'Why is increasing the UART buffer not always a throughput fix?',
 'It increases burst tolerance but cannot solve a sustained producer rate above consumption capacity. Measure rates and latency, then apply flow control, admission limits or a defined loss policy.',
 ['Framing errors and electrical UART errors are different. A valid UART byte stream can still violate your application protocol. Maintain separate counters so the first failing layer can be located.','The example borrows one internal line buffer. Calling feed_char again can overwrite a completed result; synchronous consumption or an explicit copy/ownership handoff is part of the API contract.'],
 'At 115200 8N1, the theoretical rate is 11,520 bytes/s. A 32-byte buffer provides only about 2.78 ms at that rate before accounting for parser work, and its usable capacity may be smaller. Define your acceptable burst rather than assuming a terminal sends slowly.',
 ['Feed synthetic commands on F401 first, then route UART bytes to the same function.','Count complete, rejected and transport-error outcomes separately.','Send an overlong line followed by a valid short line and verify recovery.'],
 ['Reuse the same parser on H743 behind interrupt RX first.','Only change the transport adapter when adding DMA, retaining the same parser fixtures.','Ensure DMA buffer ownership is resolved before feeding bytes and that completed line storage remains valid for its consumer.'],
 'If commands work individually but fail in bursts, inspect service latency and ownership. If overlong tails become commands, fix discard/resynchronization state rather than simply enlarging the array.');
topic('I2C and SPI transactions with bounded failure handling',
 'A bus transaction needs electrical configuration, device protocol and software timing. I2C uses addressed transfers and acknowledgment; SPI shifts data under a clock and a device-specific chip-select protocol. Read the sensor datasheet for command format, timing, multi-byte behavior and failure handling.',
 'A sensor identity read is a good first transaction: it has a known expected value and a small waveform. A successful controller transfer does not prove the returned bytes represent the intended sensor register.',
 'Transaction contract\nBus mode / speed / voltage:\nDevice address or CS polarity:\nCommand and register width:\nBytes clocked / expected response:\nTimeout and retry bound:\nOwnership of bus and RX/TX buffers:\nError and recovery state:',
 ['Choose a real compatible sensor and obtain its datasheet before wiring.','Verify pull-ups for I2C or SPI mode/CS timing for the chosen device.','Read a documented identity register with a bounded timeout.','Inject absent-device behavior and verify the application continues rather than blocking forever.'],
 'The captured transaction matches the device command/address convention, the identity value matches the datasheet, and absent hardware yields a bounded reported error.',
 'Design a serialized bus-access API that prevents two callers from interleaving one device transaction.',
 'Why can an I2C address appear shifted in a HAL call?',
 'APIs differ in whether they accept the 7-bit address or its left-shifted wire-format position. Check the exact HAL/API contract and sensor address, rather than shifting until one test happens to work.',
 ['For SPI, receive data often requires transmitting dummy bytes to generate clocks; command and data phases may share one asserted CS interval. For I2C, a register read may require a repeated-start sequence. The device protocol defines the transaction.','Treat a timeout as a state transition, not permission for infinite retries. Establish whether the peripheral/bus is idle, who owns recovery and when another client may try. Physical stuck-bus recovery requires the device-specific electrical and controller procedure.'],
 'If a sensor returns 0xFF repeatedly, possible explanations include an unselected SPI device, wrong sampling edge or an input held high. Compare command, CS, clock and data waveforms before concluding the sensor contains 0xFF.',
 ['Use F401 Cube-generated I2C or SPI pins from the actual board connector and a named compatible sensor.','Run a documented identity read, save the waveform and record the exact API address/command representation.','Disconnect the sensor in a powered-down setup, then verify bounded missing-device behavior after reconnecting power.'],
 ['Generate H743 pin and kernel-clock configuration for the same sensor contract.','Start with blocking bounded low-rate transactions, then compare interrupt/DMA only if required.','With DMA, add memory reachability/cache ownership without changing CS or repeated-start semantics.'],
 'If no sensor is available, mark physical results not run and test a fake adapter; do not label synthetic bytes as an observed hardware identity.');
topic('RTOS tasks, queues and ownership boundaries',
 'An RTOS schedules ready tasks according to its configured policy and priorities. A blocked task waits for an event without consuming normal CPU execution time. Design tasks around ownership and latency requirements, not one task per function. Queues transfer copies or explicitly defined references; they do not automatically extend object lifetime.',
 'A sensor task produces samples, a control task owns alarm state, and a telemetry task sends reports. Queue full behavior must be defined so a disconnected terminal cannot block the control path indefinitely.',
 '/* FreeRTOS-style API sketch: create queue/tasks before scheduling. */\n/* QueueHandle_t q = xQueueCreate(8, sizeof(sample_t));\n   Producer task: xQueueSend(q, &owned_sample, bounded_ticks);\n   Consumer task: xQueueReceive(q, &local_sample, portMAX_DELAY);\n   ISR: use permitted ...FromISR API and port yield convention. */\n/* Configure syscall priorities for the selected port.\n   This sketch is not a complete FreeRTOS project. */',
 ['Draw task owners, queue payloads, priorities and blocking points.','Define queue-full policy and maximum response latency.','Create a minimal RTOS project using its supported STM32 integration.','Measure stack usage, queue high-water behavior and latency under a delayed consumer.'],
 'The producer/consumer protocol has bounded overload behavior, and lower-priority telemetry cannot indefinitely stop control updates.',
 'Compare queueing copied samples with queueing pointers to a fixed buffer pool; specify who returns each buffer.',
 'What is the practical difference between a mutex and a binary semaphore?',
 'A mutex represents ownership and typically supports priority inheritance under defined conditions; a binary semaphore is a signaling/count-limited synchronization primitive. Use the RTOS documented semantics, including which operations are permitted from an ISR.',
 ['Priorities do not make shared state safe. A high-priority task can preempt a lower-priority owner midway through an update. Use single ownership, suitable queues or appropriately scoped synchronization.','Tick delays are scheduling units, not precise waveform generators. Use hardware timers for accurate peripheral timing and measure task response under realistic interrupt and task load.'],
 'If a queue holds eight samples and production is ten samples/s while the consumer stops for one second, ten arrivals exceed capacity. Decide whether to drop newest, overwrite a latest-value mailbox, or apply bounded backpressure based on the product requirement.',
 ['Use a supported F401 FreeRTOS configuration with one producer and one blocking consumer queue.','Artificially delay the consumer and record queue-full returns rather than ignoring them.','Inspect each task stack watermark with the correct port/API units.'],
 ['Repeat on one H743 M7 core with a supported port and documented interrupt syscall priorities.','Keep queued samples copied by value initially to isolate scheduling from DMA cache ownership.','Only use inter-core communication after designing a separate shared-memory ownership/cache protocol.'],
 'If a task never runs, inspect ready/blocked state, priority, scheduler startup and stack allocation. If an ISR API asserts, verify FromISR usage and permitted priority range rather than disabling assertions.');
topic('Watchdogs and recovery as a product behavior',
 'A watchdog detects a missing progress guarantee, not every possible defect. Feeding it unconditionally from a timer can conceal a dead application. Define which components must demonstrate progress before a supervisor refreshes it, and design startup and safe outputs for the reset that follows.',
 'A gateway can require both input processing and control-state progress during a supervision window. A telemetry stall may be recoverable without reset, while a stalled control path can require a watchdog restart.',
 'Supervision design\nRequired progress participants:\nWindow and deadline:\nHow progress is reported:\nRefresh owner:\nSafe output state before/after reset:\nReset-reason and crash-record capture:\nMaximum repeated-boot policy:',
 ['Define a progress contract and one refresh owner.','Configure a device-supported watchdog interval from its actual clock assumptions.','Inject a main/application stall while keeping a timer interrupt alive.','Verify reset reason, safe startup and recovery with the debugger detached as well as attached.'],
 'A required stalled component prevents refresh and causes the intended recovery. An unrelated periodic ISR cannot keep the broken application alive forever.',
 'Design a repeated-reset counter that avoids an endless unsafe boot loop and validates retained data before use.',
 'Why is a watchdog not a substitute for timeouts?',
 'Local timeouts bound individual operations and permit targeted recovery. A watchdog is a broader supervision backstop; relying on reset for every peripheral fault can lose state and mask the actual error rate.',
 ['The independent watchdog timebase can have tolerance. Calculate the interval using the device documentation and verify observed bounds; do not promise precision from a nominal oscillator value.','Debug freeze can alter watchdog behavior while halted. A successful debugger run does not prove detached operation or recovery from a real application stall.'],
 'If a control component must report every 100 ms, choose a supervision window and watchdog interval with justified scheduling/clock margin. Document why a missed report causes reset and which short temporary delays are permitted.',
 ['Use an isolated F401 image with an explicit supervisor and a deliberately stalled main component.','Verify the watchdog refresh stops even though a timer ISR continues.','Capture reset cause before clearing it and confirm safe post-reset output initialization.'],
 ['Build the supervision policy on H743 using its documented watchdog/reset-domain behavior.','Test the selected core ownership of refresh; another core must not silently feed the same watchdog without satisfying the policy.','Repeat detached from the debugger and record measured reset latency.'],
 'If reset never occurs, inspect debug-freeze settings and unexpected refresh sites. If reset occurs too soon, check actual watchdog clock/dividers and the longest legitimate startup phase.');
topic('Bootloaders and interruption-tolerant firmware updates',
 'An update system must decide which image is valid and bootable even when programming is interrupted. Separate image staging, validation, activation and confirmation. Checksums detect accidental corruption; authenticity requires a trusted verification mechanism. Flash erase/program geometry and bank behavior are device-specific.',
 'A two-slot design downloads into an inactive slot, validates it, marks a candidate, boots it and confirms successful startup. If the candidate cannot confirm, a policy can fall back to a prior valid image. This requires sufficient flash and a carefully designed metadata state machine.',
 'Conceptual update states\nEMPTY -> RECEIVING -> VERIFIED -> CANDIDATE -> CONFIRMED\nInterrupted RECEIVING: keep old boot choice\nInvalid verification: reject candidate\nUnconfirmed boot: apply bounded retry/fallback policy\nMetadata updates: design for interrupted writes and erase units',
 ['Document flash map, erase units and boot/application boundaries for the exact MCU.','Model update metadata on a host before writing target flash.','Inject interruption after every modeled transition and verify a recoverable boot choice.','Plan a target experiment only in a reserved region with a known recovery/debug path.'],
 'Every modeled interruption leaves a valid old image or a documented recovery mode; an incomplete image is never selected as confirmed.',
 'Design metadata records with version, length, integrity and commit state; explain how torn writes are rejected.',
 'Does a CRC make an update trustworthy?',
 'It detects many accidental corruptions but does not authenticate an adversarial image. Authentication needs a trusted signature/MAC scheme and key-management policy, plus validation of length, version and allowed memory ranges.',
 ['Do not assume an application can overwrite the flash bank it is executing from without constraints. Read device-specific flash programming, stalls, bank and cache requirements. A generic copy loop is not a complete update design.','A handoff must define stack, vector table, interrupts, peripheral state and runtime startup expectations. A reset-based boot flow can simplify parts of that contract but still needs validated selection metadata.'],
 'Suppose power fails after all image bytes arrive but before validation is committed. Boot selection must still treat it as unverified. Deriving validity from “download offset reached image size” skips integrity/authenticity and final metadata commit.',
 ['Inspect F401 flash capacity/sector geometry and create a paper map; do not assume room for two full application images.','Run the host metadata fault-injection model with target flash geometry represented.','Document an external staging or recovery alternative if capacity prevents a dual-slot plan.'],
 ['Inspect the exact H743 flash organization and available image/staging capacity.','Model erase/program interruption and candidate confirmation before any target flash mutation.','For dual-core images, define compatible image-set activation rather than independently updating arbitrary core versions.'],
 'If you cannot guarantee a valid boot choice after an interrupted metadata write, redesign the commit protocol before attempting target deployment.');
topic('Power-aware firmware and low-power observability',
 'Low-power design includes wake sources, clocks, retained state, peripheral ownership and external-device behavior. A sleeping CPU does not guarantee low board current. Debug probes, regulators, LEDs and external modules can dominate consumption. Measure the actual product state and define wake/reinitialization requirements.',
 'A battery sensor wakes periodically, samples, transmits and returns to sleep. Energy depends on current multiplied by time in each phase, including retries and startup. A short high-current burst can dominate a long low-current sleep.',
 '/* CMSIS instruction illustration, not a complete low-power setup. */\n/* Configure the intended sleep depth and wake sources first. */\n__WFI();\n/* Verify device-specific clock restoration and peripheral state\n   before treating wake as normal application continuation. */',
 ['List required wake events and state that must survive.','Measure active, waiting and intended sleep states separately.','Verify every wake path restores the required clock/peripheral contracts.','Repeat without debugger assistance and account for board-level loads.'],
 'The firmware wakes from intended events, resumes with correct timing and has measured state-specific current rather than an assumed datasheet-only total.',
 'Estimate average current for 10 mA active for 10 ms and 10 microamps asleep for 990 ms, then identify omitted startup/radio costs.',
 'Why might a low-power test work only with a debugger?',
 'Debug configuration can keep clocks or domains active, alter sleep entry and reset behavior, and add board current. Validate detached behavior and record probe/power connections and device debug settings.',
 ['Define whether wake resumes context or follows a reset-like startup path for the selected mode. Retention, RAM validity and timer behavior vary by mode and device.','Avoid measuring current through an interface path that bypasses or excludes part of the board unintentionally. Record supply point, instrument bandwidth and which loads remain connected.'],
 'The ideal weighted average is (10 mA*10 ms + 0.01 mA*990 ms)/1000 ms = 0.1099 mA, or 109.9 microamps. This excludes transition and external-device costs and is an estimate to compare with measurement.',
 ['Choose a documented F401 sleep mode and timer/external wake source for a first reversible lab.','Measure wake timing and verify a heartbeat resumes without assuming SysTick behavior in sleep.','Record board LEDs, probe and regulator contribution to measured current.'],
 ['Choose a documented H743 mode and record domain/clock retention and wake-source constraints.','Verify clock restoration before interpreting UART baud or cycle timing after wake.','On dual-core parts, include the other core state in the power-state contract.'],
 'If current remains high, verify sleep entry and board loads. If wake corrupts timing, inspect timebase and clock restoration before changing application deadlines.');
topic('Driver APIs and maintainable firmware architecture',
 'A driver should expose a clear contract for initialization, operation, completion, errors and ownership. Keep board pin/clock selection separate from device protocol and application policy. An abstraction is useful when it makes a dependency testable or a contract explicit, rather than merely hiding every HAL call behind another name.',
 'A sensor module can consume a bus transaction interface while returning a typed value and error status. The same parser/conversion logic can be tested with a fake bus and used with F401 or H743 adapters.',
 '#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\ntypedef enum { IO_OK, IO_TIMEOUT, IO_INVALID } io_status_t;\ntypedef io_status_t (*read_fn)(void *ctx, uint8_t reg,\n                               uint8_t *dst, size_t n);\ntypedef struct { void *ctx; read_fn read; } sensor_bus_t;\n/* Define synchronous vs asynchronous ownership in the API. */',
 ['Draw board support, transport, device driver and application boundaries.','Define status codes and timeout units for one interface.','Implement a fake transport returning known bytes and a timeout.','Verify application policy without touching registers.'],
 'Pure conversion/policy code runs with a fake adapter, and board-specific changes stay within documented hardware boundaries.',
 'Refactor a sensor read so failure cannot partially update the application value; document reentrancy and caller context.',
 'When should a driver expose asynchronous completion?',
 'When operation latency or throughput justifies it and callers can honor buffer lifetime, cancellation and completion rules. A synchronous bounded API may be simpler for low-rate work; choose from requirements rather than MCU capability alone.',
 ['Error semantics belong in the contract: timeout, absent device, invalid response and buffer overflow require different diagnosis. Avoid returning one success boolean when callers need different recovery actions.','Document allowed contexts and shared resources. A function that blocks or takes a mutex is not automatically safe in an ISR. Global internal storage can make a seemingly stateless API non-reentrant.'],
 'A fake adapter returns a two-byte identity response on call one and IO_TIMEOUT on call two. The driver should publish the first result, preserve it but mark freshness appropriately after failure, and let application policy decide the alarm.',
 ['Build the pure driver test on a host, then bind a F401 Cube HAL adapter.','Keep board pins/handles in board-support code, not the value-conversion function.','Test timeout and invalid-response paths with the same public API.'],
 ['Bind a separate H743 adapter without modifying protocol fixtures.','Document DMA completion/cache responsibilities inside the transport boundary if asynchronous operation is used.','Review whether multiple tasks or cores can call the same instance and define serialization.'],
 'If every board change touches application logic, inspect misplaced hardware assumptions. If fake tests pass but the board fails, measure the adapter and electrical transaction rather than rewriting the pure conversion first.');
topic('Security boundaries and trustworthy device behavior',
 'Start a security design with assets, adversary capabilities and trust boundaries. Validate external inputs, protect update authenticity and define debug/manufacturing lifecycle access. Security mechanisms depend on the exact device; a software check without a protected trust root may not defend against an attacker who can replace the checker.',
 'A field maintenance port accepts commands. Bounds checks prevent accidental corruption, but privileged commands also need an authentication/authorization policy, replay handling where relevant and safe failure behavior.',
 'Threat-model worksheet\nAssets: keys, firmware, measurements, actuation\nEntry points: UART, bus, update, debug, physical access\nTrust roots and immutable/protected components:\nAllowed operations per lifecycle state:\nInput limits / authenticity / rollback policy:\nRecovery and manufacturing procedure:',
 ['List assets and realistic attacker access for a chosen device.','Identify every external parser and privileged command.','Separate integrity checking from authenticity and authorization.','Map required controls to exact MCU capabilities and operational recovery needs.'],
 'The design states which threats are covered, which controls enforce them and which assumptions remain outside scope.',
 'Design a signed-update policy with version handling and a recovery path, without inventing a custom cryptographic algorithm.',
 'Why not simply disable debug as the first security step?',
 'Debug policy is part of a lifecycle and recovery design. Device protection changes can be consequential and do not replace input validation or trusted updates. Plan manufacturing, servicing and threat coverage before changing protection state.',
 ['Use maintained cryptographic implementations and documented device capabilities. Correct algorithms can still fail through key exposure, incorrect verification order, replay or bypassable boot flow.','Do not log secrets in diagnostic records. A useful failure report can preserve build identity and error classification without exposing private keys or sensitive payloads.'],
 'A packet with a correct CRC but an unauthorized command is intact, not authorized. A signed image for the wrong hardware can also be authentic yet unsuitable; validate target identity, bounds and policy before activation.',
 ['Review F401 external interfaces and draft a threat model without changing option bytes or protection settings.','Add negative parser tests for oversized and unauthorized commands in a host harness.','Document available protection features from the exact device manual and the recovery consequences.'],
 ['Review the exact H743 device capabilities rather than assuming all H7 parts provide the same secure-boot/key features.','Separate trusted boot/update design from optional application checks.','Test policy rejection with synthetic metadata, leaving irreversible protection configuration out of this teaching lab.'],
 'If a proposed control can be bypassed by replacing ordinary writable firmware, identify the missing trust boundary rather than claiming secure boot from a signature check alone.');
topic('Reproducible builds and automated firmware delivery',
 'A reproducible engineering result ties source, toolchain, configuration, generated files and test fixtures to an identifiable artifact. CI can compile, run host tests and archive symbols; it does not automatically validate electrical behavior or target timing. Keep production artifacts and hardware evidence linked by build ID.',
 'A parser correction can pass host boundary tests in CI, build both target configurations and archive ELF/map files. A hardware runner then flashes those exact artifacts and reports sequence-loss and recovery tests under a named board configuration.',
 'Delivery manifest\nSource commit and dirty-state policy:\nToolchain and dependency versions:\nTarget / linker / generated configuration:\nBuild flags and feature options:\nArtifact hashes and build ID:\nHost results / target results / untested cases:',
 ['Build from a clean checkout with documented toolchain inputs.','Run pure-function tests before generating target artifacts.','Archive ELF, map, binary and configuration under one identity.','Separate host, compile-only and hardware results in the release record.'],
 'Another engineer can recreate the configuration and select the exact symbols for a reported build; compile success is never labeled a physical board pass.',
 'Design CI stages for two boards plus a hardware test queue, including behavior when the physical runner is offline.',
 'What should be archived besides a flash binary?',
 'Matching ELF/debug symbols, map, toolchain/configuration identity, source commit, checksums and relevant tests/stimuli. A raw binary alone loses much of the information required for fault analysis.',
 ['Pin dependencies and document generated configuration rather than assuming an IDE workspace captures everything. Deterministic artifact identity and bit-for-bit reproducibility are related but distinct claims; only claim identical output after comparing it.','Keep credentials out of source, command output and artifacts. Signing or deployment permissions should be scoped to the delivery action; a parser test job does not need production credentials.'],
 'A test matrix row can say “F401 compile: pass; F401 hardware: not run; H743 compile: pass; H743 DMA stress: pending.” This accurately expresses partial evidence rather than collapsing it to one green release label.',
 ['Document the F401 compiler, firmware package, .ioc, linker file and flags.','Rebuild from a clean directory and compare artifact identity using a recorded procedure.','Attach host tests and mark actual target experiments separately.'],
 ['Build H743 with its own linker/startup/clock configuration in the same pipeline structure.','Archive a separate ELF and map; never use F401 symbols for H7 crashes.','If a hardware runner is unavailable, report target validation pending while preserving the exact artifact to test later.'],
 'If a clean build differs, investigate timestamps, paths, generated files and dependency versions before claiming reproducibility. If CI only checks website JavaScript, do not describe it as compiling target firmware.');

topic('ADC measurements: from raw codes to meaningful samples',
 'An ADC converts an analog input into a code under a defined reference, resolution, sampling time and channel configuration. The code is not automatically a calibrated physical quantity. Source impedance, sample acquisition time, noise and reference variation can affect the measurement; timestamp and validity belong with the converted value.',
 'A battery monitor measures a divided voltage. First convert the ADC code to pin voltage using the documented reference, then apply the resistor ratio and calibration. Verify the maximum possible pin voltage and input limits before connecting the battery.',
 '#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\nbool code_to_mv(uint32_t code, uint32_t full_scale,\n                uint32_t reference_mv, uint32_t *out) {\n    if (!out || full_scale == 0U || code > full_scale ||\n        reference_mv == 0U) return false;\n    uint64_t numerator=(uint64_t)code * reference_mv;\n    *out=(uint32_t)((numerator + full_scale/2U) / full_scale);\n    return true;\n}\n/* Educational full-scale endpoint model; use device calibration\n   and transfer-function guidance for a production measurement. */',
 ['Choose an available ADC channel and verify analog input limits, pin routing and reference configuration.','Test conversion arithmetic with synthetic zero, midscale and full-scale codes.','Sample a known safe source using the device-required calibration/setup procedure.','Compare reported voltage with an independent measurement and record noise, sample time and reference assumptions.'],
 'Synthetic endpoints produce zero and reference millivolts. Physical results include measured reference/error and are not labeled precision measurements merely because they have many digits.',
 'Design a divider measurement with explicit resistor tolerance, maximum input, sampling rate, filtering and an out-of-range policy.',
 'Why can averaging fail to correct an ADC measurement?',
 'It can reduce some random variation but does not remove a wrong reference, gain/offset error, inadequate acquisition, clipping or aliasing. Identify the error source and timing/analog contract before choosing a filter.',
 ['Use wide intermediate arithmetic when multiplying a code by reference or divider factors. Avoid applying a 12-bit denominator to an ADC configured at another resolution or with hardware oversampling shifts.','A sampling rate must reflect the signal bandwidth and filtering requirement. Software averaging after aliasing cannot recover the lost signal; account for analog bandwidth and sample timing first.'],
 'In the educational 12-bit endpoint model, full_scale=4095 and reference=3300 mV yields 0 mV at code 0, approximately 1650 mV near midscale, and 3300 mV at 4095. A nominal 2:1 divider doubles inferred source voltage but also adds resistor/reference uncertainty.',
 ['Select an F401 analog-capable input from its board schematic and generate the ADC configuration for its actual reference and resolution.','Begin with bounded single conversions from a documented safe source; do not leave the input floating as an accuracy test.','Compare raw code and millivolt conversion with a meter and record acquisition time.'],
 ['Generate H743 ADC configuration including its device-required clock, calibration and input setup.','Use the actual configured resolution rather than F401 assumptions.','Only add timer-triggered DMA after the single-sample baseline is understood, with correct DMA RAM/cache handoff.'],
 'If values are stuck at zero/full scale, inspect routing, range, channel and acquisition setup. If DMA samples look stale only on M7, separate cache ownership from analog calibration.');
topic('Hardware timers, PWM and deterministic sampling',
 'A timer counts its selected clock after a prescaler and generates events at configured periods or compare values. PWM duty, update frequency and input capture are related uses of timer hardware, but their timing and register-update rules differ. Use hardware triggering when a periodic waveform or sampling instant must not inherit superloop scheduling jitter.',
 'A dimmable LED can use a timer PWM output, while a periodic ADC trigger can use a timer event. Their timing should continue under normal application load without toggling pins through HAL_Delay calls.',
 '/* Edge-aligned up-counting teaching formula: */\n/* update_hz = timer_clock_hz / ((PSC+1) * (ARR+1)) */\n/* Example only: 84 MHz / (84 * 1000) = 1000 Hz\n   PSC=83, ARR=999.\n   PWM compare near 250 gives about 25% duty in a suitable PWM mode.\n   Verify polarity, channel mode and preload/update semantics. */',
 ['Derive the actual timer input clock from the exact RCC configuration.','Choose prescaler and period with explicit integer rounding.','Route a timer channel to a documented pin and measure frequency/duty.','Add application/interrupt load and compare hardware waveform timing with a software-toggle baseline.'],
 'The measured PWM period/duty agrees with the chosen timer mode and arithmetic; application workload does not substitute itself for the hardware period.',
 'Use a timer event to schedule sampling, then explain the distinction between sample-trigger timing and delayed software processing.',
 'Why can a timer clock differ from its APB bus clock?',
 'STM32 clock trees can apply device-specific timer clock multipliers or selectable clock paths depending on bus dividers and timer configuration. Read the actual clock tree rather than equating timer frequency with an APB value.',
 ['The plus-one terms matter because a prescaler register value and a reload register value usually encode ranges rather than direct divisor/period counts. Center-aligned modes and special timer features change the simple formula.','Preload can defer new period/compare values until a controlled update event, avoiding some malformed intermediate cycles. Consult the exact timer mode and update rules; do not assume every write takes effect immediately.'],
 'For an actual 84 MHz timer clock, PSC=83 produces a 1 MHz counter. ARR=999 creates 1000 counts per edge-aligned period, so the period is 1 ms. A compare of 250 gives a nominal 250-microsecond active region in the specified PWM mode, subject to its polarity/boundary semantics.',
 ['Use a spare F401 timer channel/pin selected from the board schematic; verify its actual timer clock.','Generate a modest PWM, measure output and change compare through documented preload behavior.','Stress the main loop while confirming the timer waveform still meets its contract.'],
 ['Choose an H743 timer and derive its clock using the H7 clock tree rather than reusing F401 prescaler values.','Measure the same intended PWM frequency and duty with appropriate pin routing.','If triggering ADC/DMA, verify the trigger mapping and distinguish acquisition timing from buffer-consumer latency.'],
 'If frequency is exactly doubled or halved, inspect timer clock selection and counting mode. If duty glitches during updates, inspect preload, update generation and ownership of compare writes.');

const learningPath = [
 {title:'Hardware and C foundations',ids:[25,26,27,28,29]},
 {title:'Bring-up and the build-to-board workflow',ids:[5,30,31,8,9,6,7]},
 {title:'Interfaces, protocols and data movement',ids:[16,32,42,33,34,20]},
 {title:'Time, events and concurrent execution',ids:[43,17,18,19,35]},
 {title:'Memory safety and fault forensics',ids:[14,15,10,11,12,13]},
 {title:'Evidence-driven troubleshooting',ids:[1,2,3,4]},
 {title:'Verification, recovery and field resilience',ids:[21,22,23,36,37,38]},
 {title:'Architecture, security and delivery',ids:[39,40,41,24]}
];
const revisedTitles = {
 1:'Turn a symptom into a measurable investigation',
 2:'Build repeatable stimuli and failure records',
 3:'Locate the first broken system boundary',
 4:'Prove the mechanism and validate the correction',
 5:'Connect the probe and understand core visibility',
 6:'Control execution without mistaking halt for runtime',
 7:'Find unexpected writers with data watchpoints',
 8:'Read the binary: symbols, sections and instructions',
 9:'Keep optimized firmware observable and correct',
 10:'Understand exception routing and fault escalation',
 11:'Recover interrupted context from a validated stack frame',
 12:'Decode fault evidence without trusting stale addresses',
 13:'Trace a crash address to the actual memory operation',
 14:'Protect buffer bounds and object ownership',
 15:'Budget stacks and diagnose allocation failures',
 16:'Trace configuration from clocks to connector pins',
 17:'Follow an interrupt from event to acknowledgment',
 18:'Event ownership and lost-update races',
 19:'Measure response time and deadline behavior',
 20:'Design UART and DMA buffer handoffs',
 21:'Test contracts at boundaries and rejection paths',
 22:'Exercise recovery with controlled failures',
 23:'Make each correction a lasting regression case',
 24:'Deliver a reproducible engineering case study'
};
for (const l of lessons) if (revisedTitles[l.id]) l.title=revisedTitles[l.id];
modules.splice(0,modules.length,...learningPath.map(p=>p.title));
const orderedLessons=learningPath.flatMap((p,module)=>p.ids.map(id=>{
 const l=lessons.find(item=>item.id===id);
 if(!l) throw new Error(`Missing course lesson ${id}`);
 l.module=module;
 return l;
}));
lessons.splice(0,lessons.length,...orderedLessons);
lessons.forEach((l,index)=>{l.position=index+1;});
